[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that would be a good thing to clarify.

530
00:48:28,084 --> 00:48:37,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you think would be, like, a good measure of... just some example of an action, when it's just chanda, and when it's just... when it's actually craving?

531
00:48:37,933 --> 00:48:38,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

532
00:48:38,234 --> 00:48:39,795
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you be able to tell for yourself?

533
00:48:39,935 --> 00:48:40,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, externally,

534
00:48:41,877 --> 00:48:54,166
[SPEAKER_01]: It might look the same, like, some people might say, oh, some people even blame the Buddha, the ascetics and stuff, when he saw, when they saw him eating, like, normal food, they thought, oh, he's just acting out of craving.

535
00:48:54,727 --> 00:49:10,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So, since you cannot measure it by the external standards, necessarily, unless the person is, like, breaking precepts and stuff, how would you be able to tell for yourself, internally, what is more, like, just chanda, and what is clearly craving?

536
00:49:13,695 --> 00:49:22,262
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, because in one sense, if you see that it's craving, you are understanding it, and so you are going more towards chanda.

537
00:49:22,422 --> 00:49:23,183
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh...

538
00:49:23,763 --> 00:49:24,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But what...

539
00:49:24,124 --> 00:49:30,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Since there is ignorance... What is... what is there when there is craving?

540
00:49:31,510 --> 00:49:36,994
[SPEAKER_00]: The action, the wanting, the pressure... And what is... and how is that felt?

541
00:49:38,294 --> 00:49:38,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant.

542
00:49:38,915 --> 00:49:39,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

543
00:49:39,375 --> 00:49:40,175
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's suffering.

544
00:49:41,276 --> 00:49:44,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, when there is craving, as always, there's going to be suffering.

545
00:49:44,579 --> 00:50:03,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the main kind of practical rule of thumb would be, if something is rooted in craving, or if you have a desire, let's say, you have an interest to do something, if that interest is infected with craving, the effect of that is going to be, if you don't do it,

546
00:50:05,117 --> 00:50:05,777
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna suffer.

547
00:50:06,017 --> 00:50:06,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

548
00:50:06,938 --> 00:50:10,699
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's just chanda, then you don't do it.

549
00:50:11,379 --> 00:50:13,100
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not gonna... it's not gonna be a big deal.

550
00:50:13,520 --> 00:50:15,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no passion involved.

551
00:50:15,581 --> 00:50:16,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is...

552
00:50:16,942 --> 00:50:20,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's how you would measure the presence of passion or not.

553
00:50:20,403 --> 00:50:23,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, not by necessarily kind of

554
00:50:26,828 --> 00:50:41,935
[SPEAKER_01]: The external qualities of the action, let's say, like how much you did or how much effort you put in, it's more like how much would you have suffered if you couldn't do it.

555
00:50:42,255 --> 00:50:43,896
[SPEAKER_04]: And that is on the level of reflection.

556
00:50:44,276 --> 00:50:47,458
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not that you actually need to do it or not do it.

557
00:50:48,696 --> 00:50:49,637
[SPEAKER_04]: That's already secondary.

558
00:50:49,677 --> 00:50:53,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it would be based on pretty much recognition and self-honesty.

559
00:50:53,980 --> 00:50:58,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, this mind is like a trained animal.

560
00:50:59,064 --> 00:51:08,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, it wants to kind of get up and do something, but if you don't let it, it's not that it's going to start barking at you and stuff.

561
00:51:09,071 --> 00:51:11,873
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that would be one practical way to realize, like,

562
00:51:13,034 --> 00:51:33,148
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, as the Buddha would say, eating just for the sake of sustaining the body, for example, or using the lodgings, or using the robe just for the sake of the minimal survival, then that would be like, oh, you know, am I having... would I be bothered if I can't eat today?

563
00:51:34,749 --> 00:51:39,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I be... or would I be bothered if I can't get the food that... the specific food that I want to get?

564
00:51:41,148 --> 00:51:53,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, see, even if the answer is no, and the mind doesn't really care, that doesn't mean that you wouldn't still try to find something to eat.

565
00:51:55,196 --> 00:51:58,157
[SPEAKER_01]: The point being, if you don't get anything, you're not going to suffer.

566
00:51:58,738 --> 00:52:00,879
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's the poems in the suttas.

567
00:52:00,919 --> 00:52:06,562
[SPEAKER_01]: It would say, like, the monks back in the day, like, whether he gets something in anāryānta or not,

568
00:52:08,114 --> 00:52:09,014
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't be affected.

569
00:52:09,315 --> 00:52:09,995
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

570
00:52:10,035 --> 00:52:10,736
[SPEAKER_04]: There's this line.

571
00:52:10,836 --> 00:52:16,300
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not sure how accurately it is translated, but it says somewhere, he expects only what is given.

572
00:52:18,242 --> 00:52:20,284
[SPEAKER_04]: But you don't know, of course, beforehand what is given.

573
00:52:20,304 --> 00:52:22,465
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how can you expect it?

574
00:52:22,886 --> 00:52:24,487
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's a nice way of putting it.

575
00:52:24,967 --> 00:52:25,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

576
00:52:25,368 --> 00:52:28,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you could say, oh, look, he went to get it.

577
00:52:28,810 --> 00:52:30,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can that not be rooted in craving?

578
00:52:31,753 --> 00:52:36,337
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's because, again, it's not rooted in that external kind of qualities of the action.

579
00:52:36,997 --> 00:52:44,299
[SPEAKER_01]: It's internally, how much was he emotionally invested in a certain outcome, let's say.

580
00:52:44,379 --> 00:52:46,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the ignorance is reflected.

581
00:52:46,900 --> 00:52:48,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you...

582
00:52:48,521 --> 00:52:55,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance being, let's say, fundamentally taking things in mind, not understanding the lack of ownership.

583
00:52:57,163 --> 00:53:04,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Any suffering, to any extent, is going to be rooted in the fact that you took something as yours.

584
00:53:05,798 --> 00:53:07,859
[SPEAKER_04]: Which could be, for example, this very body.

585
00:53:07,920 --> 00:53:08,200
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

586
00:53:08,520 --> 00:53:15,265
[SPEAKER_04]: If you take it as yours, then the whole relationship of food, which is what this body depends upon, becomes problematic.

587
00:53:15,785 --> 00:53:28,094
[SPEAKER_04]: But now imagine that it is, to the extent that it can be imagined, that this body is not yours, then how does that affect your relationship to food, to its necessity, to the sensuality that is bound up with it?

588
00:53:28,775 --> 00:53:33,198
[SPEAKER_04]: All those actions, all those cravings in relation to food,

589
00:53:33,952 --> 00:53:42,398
[SPEAKER_04]: will undergo, let's say, radical shift, because you now see that that has only value in relation to this body, which is seen as not yours.

590
00:53:43,239 --> 00:53:50,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so that's where the contemplation of anatta would have a valid, like a tangible effect.

591
00:53:50,464 --> 00:53:52,165
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just a philosophical idea.

592
00:53:52,966 --> 00:53:55,228
[SPEAKER_01]: It's precisely in this context of

593
00:53:56,075 --> 00:54:01,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Does the mind actually have these desires and obsessions still, or not?

594
00:54:01,639 --> 00:54:01,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

595
00:54:02,179 --> 00:54:13,127
[SPEAKER_04]: And with rope and lodgings is, of course, also directly bound up with having a body, because you want to protect yourself from elements, from sun, heat, animals and stuff, which is what the lodging does.

596
00:54:13,147 --> 00:54:15,628
[SPEAKER_04]: Lodging is basically sort of a further extension of your rope.

597
00:54:15,769 --> 00:54:19,551
[SPEAKER_04]: Your rope you have on you, but then you go into the lodging for further protection.

598
00:54:19,571 --> 00:54:20,492
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's the same principle.

599
00:54:22,573 --> 00:54:22,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

600
00:54:23,254 --> 00:54:23,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

601
00:54:24,948 --> 00:54:25,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

602
00:54:25,389 --> 00:54:33,883
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent that you understood... to the extent that you're not ignorant about things not being yours, you will not suffer when you don't get what you want.

603
00:54:33,903 --> 00:54:34,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

604
00:54:34,725 --> 00:54:35,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you try to get it.

605
00:54:36,708 --> 00:54:37,329
[SPEAKER_01]: With Chanda.

